TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "research_concepts": [
    "Pick and Roll (PNR)",
    "PNR failure",
    "Pull-up shot",
    "Offensive counter strategy",
    "Play-type efficiency",
    "Strategic adjustment",
    "Points Per Possession (PPP)"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define 'PNR failure'?",
    "How to link sequential plays (PNR to PULL_UP)?",
    "How to measure 'lift' in offensive efficiency?",
    "What is the appropriate unit of analysis for strategic insights (player, team, play)?",
    "How to establish causality or reactive patterns in basketball play?",
    "Identifying play types from play-by-play data."
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available from prior project work."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "The historical evidence does not establish any findings or limitations relevant to this research question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The availability of 'derived play-type possession records' is critical for identifying PNR and PULL_UP plays and their associated points. The play-by-play data will allow for sequential analysis within possessions or between immediately consecutive possessions.",
    "The definition of 'PNR fail' is a primary challenge, as it needs to capture a specific defensive outcome that prompts a counter, rather than just a low-scoring PNR.",
    "The 'units' likely refer to individual players, as strategic adjustments are often player-driven."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The precise definition of 'PNR fail' as it can be derived from the available data is unclear.",
    "How 'derived play-type possession records' handle sequences or secondary actions within a possession (e.g., if a PNR immediately leads to a PULL_UP shot as part of the same classified possession or as a new, distinct possession) is not specified.",
    "The exact baseline for calculating 'lift' (e.g., PULL_UP efficiency in general vs. PULL_UP efficiency in non-PNR-fail contexts) needs to be formally defined.",
    "Whether the graph database linking play types can reliably identify a 'counter' sequence as opposed to mere correlation."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No historical evidence was available, so it did not change the decision."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.6,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
        "text": "The definition of 'PNR fail' is not directly available and requires careful, potentially subjective, derivation from play-by-play data or assumptions about the 'derived play-type possession records'. This definition is central to the research question."
      },
      {
        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "The specific sequence 'PNR fail' leading to a 'PULL_UP' as a counter might be rare for many individual 'units' (players). This could lead to insufficient data points to reliably calculate 'lift' for 70% of units with useful power, especially for the '2pp' threshold."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "A PULL_UP shot following a PNR that didn't yield points might be due to various factors (e.g., shot clock pressure, general offensive scheme, player tendencies) rather than a deliberate counter-strategy to a 'failed' PNR. Isolating the 'counter' intent is challenging."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "no",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No historical evidence was available, so the question has not been answered by prior project work."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
      "text": "The definition of 'PNR fail' is crucial and must be precisely operationalized. If it's too broad (e.g., any PNR not scoring), it might not capture the intended strategic 'failure'. If too narrow, it might miss relevant instances. This definition directly impacts the label of the antecedent event."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "Many players may not have a sufficient sample size of 'PNR fail' -> 'PULL_UP' sequences to reliably estimate 'lift' with statistical power, particularly for the specified thresholds (lift > 0, median lift > 2pp). This will make it difficult to assess the '>=70% of units' criterion."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "The observed sequence 'PNR fail' -> 'PULL_UP' might be confounded by other factors such as shot clock situations, defensive adjustments not directly related to the PNR 'failure', or general offensive play calls. It's difficult to isolate the causal link of a 'counter-strategy'."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "The 'lift' measurement (points per possession) for individual units, especially for rare sequences, might be highly variable and not reliable without substantial sample sizes."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "Testing for 'lift > 0' and 'median lift > 2pp' across potentially hundreds of 'units' (players) introduces a multiple testing problem, requiring appropriate corrections to control the family-wise error rate."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.2,
    "rejected": 0.3,
    "untestable": 0.4,
    "artifact": 0.05,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The 'lift' in Points Per Possession (PPP) for PULL_UP shots immediately following a 'PNR fail' compared to PULL_UP shots in non-PNR-fail contexts. This lift will be calculated for each individual player (unit). The estimand is the proportion of players (units) for whom this lift is significantly greater than 0, and the median lift across these players is greater than 2pp.",
    "unit": "Player-season. Each player in each season (2022-23 to 2025-26) will be considered a distinct unit. A player must meet a minimum threshold of both 'PNR fail' -> 'PULL_UP' possessions and 'non-PNR-fail' PULL_UP possessions to be included in the analysis.",
    "sample": "All players from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (regular season and playoffs) who meet the minimum play count thresholds. Minimum thresholds will be determined by power analysis, but initially consider 50 possessions for each category ('PNR fail' -> 'PULL_UP' and 'non-PNR-fail' PULL_UP) per player-season unit.",
    "power": "A power analysis will be conducted to determine the minimum number of possessions required for each player-season unit to detect a 2pp lift with 80% power at alpha=0.05. This will inform the minimum sample size threshold for units. The overall power for detecting the '>=70% of units' criterion will also be assessed.",
    "controls": "The primary control is the comparison group: PULL_UP shots that do *not* immediately follow a 'PNR fail'. Further controls will include: game state (score margin, quarter/period), shot clock remaining, defensive scheme (if derivable). Player talent will be implicitly controlled by analyzing player-specific lifts. To mitigate confounding, strict temporal proximity (e.g., same possession or immediate next possession) between 'PNR fail' and 'PULL_UP' will be enforced.",
    "falsification": "The hypothesis would be falsified if significantly less than 70% of player-season units show a positive lift, or if the median lift across units with positive lift is not greater than 2pp. Additionally, if the 'lift' is found to be significant for sequences that are not logically 'counters' (e.g., PULL_UP following a successful PNR), it could indicate confounding.",
    "multiplicity": "Given multiple units, a False Discovery Rate (FDR) correction (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg) will be applied when assessing the 'lift > 0' criterion for individual units. The 'median lift > 2pp' will be assessed on the distribution of uncorrected player-level lifts.",
    "decision_rule": "The hypothesis is supported if, after applying FDR correction for individual unit tests, at least 70% of player-season units show a statistically significant positive lift (p < adjusted_alpha), AND the median of the *estimated* lifts (in PPP) for all included player-season units (or just those with positive lift) is greater than 2pp. If the sample size for 'PNR fail' -> 'PULL_UP' is too small for a substantial number of players, the question will be deemed untestable for those players.",
    "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, 2025-26 regular season and playoffs. Data will be sourced from the derived play-type possession records and supplemented by play-by-play data for sequential analysis and context. 'PNR fail' will be defined as a PNR possession that results in 0 points and is immediately followed by a PULL_UP shot by the same offensive player within the same offensive possession or as the first action of the subsequent possession by the same team."
  }
}

